  <section id="view-model" aria-label="The model" hidden>
    <div class="prose">
      <h1>The model, in full</h1>
      <p>The board's ranking is one formula applied to every row. It never names a protocol: the same arithmetic scores all of them, so a row's place is a consequence of its own record.</p>

      <div class="formula">base       = tvl_usd × multiple(category)
growth     = clamp(change_7d, −60, 120)          per cent
growthMult = 1 + (growth / 100) × 0.50
maturity   = 0.55 + min(age_days, 1095) / 1095 × 0.45
breadth    = 1 + min(chains − 1, 4) × 0.03
implied    = max(1, round(base × growthMult × maturity × breadth))

multiple(category) = (n × median(category) + 4 × median(all)) / (n + 4)</div>

      <h2>Where the multiple comes from</h2>
      <p>The house starter multiplies TVL by a flat 0.25 for every protocol. This model measures the multiple instead. Every protocol that already has a token carries a market cap and a TVL in the same snapshot, so their ratio is what the market has paid for that kind of protocol. For a pre-token protocol the board takes the median of that ratio over the live protocols in the same category, and the median over all live protocols when a category is too thin to trust.</p>
      <p>The result is a multiple that moves with the market's own pricing of each kind of protocol rather than with one house constant, and it moves when the snapshot's live peers move.</p>

      <h3>Why a category's multiple is shrunk</h3>
      <p>A category with three measured peers is a small sample, and a median of three can sit far from the market. Each category's own median is therefore pulled toward the median over all live protocols, as if four extra peers drawn from the whole population stood beside it. A category with fifteen peers barely moves; a category with three is pulled most of the way back. Where a category holds fewer than three live protocols, its own median is not used at all and the table below marks the row as a global fallback.</p>

